From d7cdbe55177121230ed2aa2c94daae26eaffdd6f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
Message-Id: <d7cdbe55177121230ed2aa2c94daae26eaffdd6f.1792497887.git.dev@kobbler.invalid>
In-Reply-To: <8bbee53215a2fe1b7845ec38b3385347f5d6aa41.1791977240.git.dev@kobbler.invalid>
References: <8bbee53215a2fe1b7845ec38b3385347f5d6aa41.1791977240.git.dev@kobbler.invalid>
From: Kobbler-Media <dev@kobbler.invalid>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 23:04:47 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 7/7] android: display: pace release times to the vsync

The display asks SurfaceFlinger to present each picture at the date the
clock gives it, and that date carries the timestamp's jitter: Matroska
stores milliseconds, so 23.976fps arrives in 41 and 42ms steps.
SurfaceFlinger presents a buffer on the first vsync whose expected present
time is past the requested one, and when an app votes a frame rate it may
latch that app's buffers only on vsyncs at that rate. A Pixel 6 Pro playing
24p at 120Hz takes the app's buffers every fifth vsync: requests sitting a
fraction of a millisecond either side of one wait a whole frame, and the
next two pictures land together, one dropped. The panel runs slightly fast,
so the requests drift across that boundary every few seconds and each pass
left a burst of held and dropped frames.

As Media3's VideoFrameReleaseHelper does, fit the timestamps to the frame
duration found in them, and move each release onto the vsync it is closest
to, most of a period ahead of it, with hysteresis between two candidates.
The vsync timeline comes from the NDK Choreographer, sampled every half
second on a thread of its own; without it (before Android 10) the times are
only smoothed. The same times feed releaseOutputBufferAtTime when the
decoder renders straight into the window.
